/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A mixed-member proportional system (MMP) places greater emphasis on politicians solving local issues that standard PR":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because, under a MMP system, half of the seats in government are filled by people directly elected by their local constituency, the government of MMP is able to have a moral local outlook than strict PR, which typically has a more national perspective.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the advantage that MMP has over PR.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Advantages of both over SMP:<ul><li>Cannot be gerrymandered.</li><li>Prevents minority rule.</li><li>Allows for political diversity.</li></ul></li><li>Under MMP, a voter casts two votes: a vote for a representative, and a vote for a party. <ul><li>For the vote for the representative, the representative who receives the most vote wins, and becomes the MP for that area. All of the MPs here comprise half of the parliament.</li><li>For the vote for the party, the other half of parliament is made up of MPs from the parties, with the seats divided based on the portions of votes that they received.<ul><li>The use of the “Party List,” a list of preferred candidates to receive the seats allocated for a particular party increases party loyalty.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect\">Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect</a><ul><li>Both the systems proposed in this note solve many of the problems that are found in instant-runoff and SMP.</li></ul></li></ul>","American white males are underrepresented in academia and media in lower-level positions, and overrepresented in upper level positions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Lower-level positions are increasingly being dominated by minorities and women, while upper-level positions remain dominated by white males, a trend that emerged around 2014. This was the result of established leaders protecting their own status while sacrificing the prospects of younger men to meet diversity quotas.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>As a result, many qualified professionals left their fields or remain stuck in lower-level positions.</p><h4>Examples</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Jacob Savage, <em>The Lost Generation</em></p><p>At <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LVwbrfB8qcm0SCwaLrBzlZhCcsnbT0VM/view?usp=sharing&amp;ref=compactmag.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Berkeley</a>, as recently as 2015, white male hires were 52.7 percent of new tenure-track faculty; in 2023, they were 21.5 percent. UC <a href=\"https://ap.uci.edu/new-faculty-appointments/?ref=compactmag.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Irvine</a> has hired 64 tenure-track assistant professors in the humanities and social sciences since 2020. Just three (4.7 percent) are white men. Of the 59 Assistant Professors in Arts, Humanities and Social Science appointed at UC Santa Cruz between 2020-2024, only two were white men (3 percent).</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","Animalism regarding identity states that humans are merely biological animals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that humans are nothing but the biological material that make then up. If the physical body is destroyed, the self is likewise destroyed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant views in personal identity.</p><h4>References</h4>","Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Economists tend the overestimate the negative effects of tariffs, since in theory, they are detrimental to the economy. However, other factors may make it so that the effects of tariffs are not as extremely as originally predicted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant since the Trump administration has implemented many tariffs, which economists claim would wreck the US economy, despite that not having happened.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several factors that may explain why the effect of tariffs may not be as bad as predicted:</p><ul><li>Specifically regarding the Trump administration’s tariffs, many of the highest tariffs have been reduced, which means that their economic effect is diminished.</li><li>Exports only make up around 11% of the American economy.</li><li>Booms in specific sectors of the economy may make up for the dent caused by tariffs.</li><li>Businesses may temporarily absorb the increased costs brought on by tariffs.</li></ul><p>However, in the longer-term, tariffs may increase uncertainty in the economy, and thus be detrimental.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul><p>-</p>","Eels":"<h4>Notes</h4><h4>References</h4>","Gerontocracies are bad":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rule by the elderly is bad, for a variety of reasons. For instance, they may not be able to keep up with contemporary developments in economics, culture, and politics, and thus not accurately represent the interests of the wider population.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Gerontocracies are increasingly the norm in democratic societies across the world, and thus note outlines a case against them.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions\">Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions</a><ul><li>Perhaps if the government consisted of more youthful members, the whole tense period leading up to the debate could have been avoided?</li></ul></li></ul>","Heritability makes intergenerational income correlation consistent with equality of opportunity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Parents and children share genes as well as circumstances, so a correlation between parental and child income is exactly what one should expect even in a society with fully equal opportunity, and the correlation alone therefore does not establish that opportunity is unequal.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the strongest empirical defence available against the standard mobility statistics, and any argument from mobility data has to answer it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mankiw presses the point in this form. The reply is that the argument proves too much, since it would equally license inferring genetic transmission from any persistent group difference, and it does not distinguish heritable traits that constitute merit from heritable traits that merely correlate with reward.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Income mobility is becoming more difficult to achieve\">Income mobility is becoming more difficult to achieve</a><ul><li>The finding this claim is designed to reinterpret.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The Rawlsian response, which grants heritability and denies it is morally relevant.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Variability Hypothesis states that males exhibit a wider spread in many traits\">The Variability Hypothesis states that males exhibit a wider spread in many traits</a><ul><li>Another argument of the same structure, and vulnerable in the same way.</li></ul></li></ul>","Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A social truth is one which is able to the challenged by others. A “truth,” even if its actually true, which is never (able to be) challenged is a sort of “accidental” truth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains one of the important functions of freedom of expression.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Therefore, one of the important functions of freedom of expression is the creation of <em>robust social truths</em>, since freedom of expression encourages people to challenge “accidental truths.” An idea/system of thought which goes unchallenged “rots” over time and loses the its soul/spark. Eventually, the disciples to the thought mere begin to rote memorize the tenants of the thought and all vitality is lost. This is also why it is typically better to learn from the real world than just from textbooks or teachers, since the real world does not merely offer strawman versions of alternative truths, but is filled with people who vehemently defend their alternative visions.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>There are <em>logical truths</em> which do not need to be challenged in order to be proven to be true. However, these truths are merely products of deduction and logic, and are not the type of social truths discussed here.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a><ul><li>This is <em>not</em> the type of truth dealt with here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition\">Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition</a><ul><li>This is essentially the principle that is advocated here, applied to metaphysics rather than epistemology.</li></ul></li></ul>","If an molecule has and O or N, bonded to C or H, it is likely polar":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>To determine polarity, use this simple method: if there is a “strong” molecule ($O$ or $N$), and they are bonded to a “weaker” molecule (that has less electronegativity), the molecule is polar.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>This is an important concept in biology, since it is important to know if a molecule is polar. For instance, The more polar a molecule, the more soluble it is in water is an example of how this Zettel can be applied.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>If a “strong” molecule bonds with another strong molecule, it is non-polar. If a \"strong\" molecule bonds with a \"weak\" molecule, then it is polar. The bonds that are formed here are covalent bonds (see Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds). Strength here is determine by electronegativity, as The more electronegative an atom, the more it wants electrons.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds\">Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The more electronegative an atom, the more negatively charged it is\">The more electronegative an atom, the more negatively charged it is</a></li></ul>","Liberality (generosity) is the result of giving to others under the right conditions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Liberality, or generosity, mean giving the right amount, at the right time, at the right place, and most importantly, to the right person. This person also does not feel pain when they engage in the act of spending/giving money.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides a definition for a virtue that Aristotle talks about.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Liberality is the virtue of wealth management, not just wealth disposal. It means governing well both the inflow and the outflow of wealth. Like all other virtues, liberality depends of the disposition of the liberal person, not on the amount or quality of things given. Additionally, of the two vices that surround liberality, illiberality and prodigality, it is better to be prodigal than illiberal, though much more people are illiberal than prodigal.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Liberality is one of these virtues that must be repeatedly practiced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth\">Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth</a><ul><li>A variation on the virtue of liberality.</li></ul></li></ul>","Long term goodness is the means to a happy life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A good life, as Aristotle argues, is also a happy one. This is because a good life is a life whereby the individual achieve their duties as a human well, and since the final end of humans actions is happiness, then goodness must eventually arrive upon happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important argument Aristotle uses in order to arrive upon the idea of goodness, especially as a means of achieving happiness.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>So, goodness it the <em>mean</em> by which we can live a happy life, which is the <em>end</em>.</p><ol><li>Virtue (goodness) → right action.</li><li>Right action → expertly fulfills the function of a rational being.</li><li>Fulling the function well → produces happiness.</li></ol><p>Therefore, goodness produces happiness.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Aristotle clarifies that one must be good in a sustained manner, as being good “\\[demands] not only complete goodness \\[for a limited period of time], but a complete life \\[of goodness].”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li></ul>","Nature is destructive and beautiful at once":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The same indifference that makes the natural world lethal is what makes it beautiful, so the two aspects are not in tension but are the same property described from two positions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is why the literature of exposure so often reads as reverent rather than hostile, and it distinguishes the natural sublime from mere danger.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>London makes the point through the manner of the death in <em>To Build A Fire</em>, which is rendered as drowsy and almost peaceful, against a landscape whose beauty is a direct function of its emptiness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humanity is insignificant\">Humanity is insignificant</a><ul><li>The premise the indifference rests on.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The world is unfair and amoral\">The world is unfair and amoral</a><ul><li>The same indifference described in moral rather than aesthetic terms.</li></ul></li></ul>","One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One should not attempt to analyze a belief in a supreme power based on rigorous, logical argumentation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a viewpoint expressed by some when it comes to topics relating to the philosophy of religion.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>An example of this in literature is in <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>, where Fyodor attempts to analyze his faithlessness with an argument about how there are no hooks in hell, since they don’t have furnaces to make iron and because there isn’t a ceiling in hell for the hooks to hang on. He seems to find a contradiction in whether hell has a ceiling or not.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Fyodor Pavlovich, <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></p><p>You see, however stupid I am about it, I keep thinking, I keep thinking — from time to time, of course, not all the while. It's impossible, I think, for the devils to forget to drag me down to hell with their hooks when I die. Then I wonder — hooks? Where would they get them? What of? Iron hooks? Where do they forge them? Have they a foundry there of some sort? The monks in the monastery probably believe that there's a ceiling in hell, for instance. Now I'm ready to believe in hell, but without a ceiling. It makes it more refined, more enlightened, more Lutheran that is. And, after all, what does it matter whether it has a ceiling or hasn't? But, do you know, there's a damnable question involved in it? If there's no ceiling there can be no hooks, and if there are no hooks it all breaks down, which is unlikely again, for then there would be none to drag me down to hell, and if they don't drag me down what justice is there in the world?</p></blockquote><p>It seems that by attempting to justify something with argumentation, one can only trivialize it. Dostoevsky seems to make the case that this is not the right way to approach religion, this sort of materialist, trivially-rationalist approach.</p><h4>References</h4>","Securing a good may increase its value, but security does not necessarily mean greater value":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Securing a good may increase its value (i.e., gold in a vault is more valuable than gold that is just lying in the open), but security does not necessarily mean value will be enhanced. Generally, the greater the inherent value of the good, the greater security may enhance its value.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the effect that security may have on a good, namely that given the good is inherently valuable, security would increase its value.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Given a good that is inherently value-less, like dirt, increased security does not mean that the value of the dirt is greater. One would not prefer dirt that is locked in an ultra secure vault compared to dirt that is scattered randomly on the ground.</p><h4>References</h4>","Since humans constitute history, the principles that undergird history must then be accessible to humans":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since humans are the ones that act in history (history is thus generated by humans), humans should consequently be able to infer the axioms that underly history.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is essentially Vico’s justification for why he is able to construct a philosophy of history.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Vico does not entirely secularize his theory, since he adds that it is through this process of discovering history that we are able to reach God. The entirely material aspects of the world is what constitutes providence, and thus, it remains entirely accessible to humans. There is no supernatural forces that are involved and which constitute providence.</p><ul><li>To me more specific, the material of history is human, meaning that since we are able to understand the way that humans think and behave and react to stimuli, we are able to understand, at the more intimate and fundamental level, the driver of human history.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>It seems that perhaps Vico’s claim is defeated by the fact that morality advances through time, and therefore, we might not be able to actually understand the way of thinking of other parties across time.</li></ul></li></ul>","Stable, successful political institutions have strong self-correcting mechanisms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All stable and successful institutions in history have had robust self-correcting institutions such that it is able to correct itself without the need for external stimuli.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core, identifying factor of successful institutions, which may help us understand why existing institutions work and design better institutions for the future.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Grouping the examples by the mechanism actually doing the correcting.</p><p><strong>Electoral and franchise correction</strong></p><ul><li>British Reform Acts of 1832, 1867, and 1884. Acemoglu and Robinson read these as elites extending the franchise under threat of revolution, because a promise to redistribute while keeping power was not credible. The 1832 Act raised the electorate from roughly 478,000 out of 24 million to 813,000, and the decades after saw radical social reform and a falling Gini in England and Wales. Pressure converted into rule change rather than rupture.</li><li>Westminster removing sitting prime ministers mid-term (Thatcher 1990, Truss 2022) without a regime crisis.</li></ul><p><strong>Constitutional amendment and judicial reversal</strong></p><ul><li>US 17th Amendment (1913) responding to legislature-appointed Senate corruption; the 13th–15th after 1865.</li><li><em>Brown v. Board</em> (1954) overturning <em>Plessy</em> (1896). Note the lag: 58 years is a slow thermostat.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-crisis institutional redesign</strong></p><ul><li>German Basic Law (1949) explicitly engineered against Weimar's failure modes: constructive vote of no confidence, 5% threshold, Constitutional Court.</li><li>Post-Watergate: FEC, FOIA amendments, Church Committee.</li><li>Pecora hearings into Glass-Steagall (1933).</li></ul><p><strong>Monitoring and low-cost dispute resolution</strong></p><ul><li>Ostrom's design principles for long-enduring commons institutions include graduated sanctions, rapid access to low-cost local arenas for conflict resolution, and nested enterprises, with monitors who are accountable to users or are the users themselves. Japanese irrigation associations are the standard case.</li></ul><p><strong>Polycentric experimentation</strong></p><ul><li>Swiss cantons plus the referendum; US federalism. Errors are localized and cheap to reverse.</li></ul><p><strong>Recent live case</strong></p><ul><li>Chile 2022: the constitutional draft was rejected at referendum, the process restarted, the state held.</li></ul><p><strong>Where I would push back on the claim</strong></p><ul><li>It risks circularity. \"Stable and successful\" is often operationalized as \"survived shocks,\" and surviving shocks is what self-correction means. To make the claim non-trivial you need a measure of correction capacity that is observable <em>ex ante</em> and predicts survival, not read off the outcome.</li><li>Survivorship bias. Weimar had elections, PR, and judicial review, and corrected nothing. Correction mechanisms are necessary-ish, not sufficient.</li><li>Correction machinery is capturable. The Roman tribunate was a genuine correction device in the Conflict of the Orders and then a tool for Clodius and Caesar.</li><li>Rival explanation: some polities were stable for centuries with weak correction and strong repression (Qing, Ottoman). Acemoglu and Robinson themselves treat persistence as often coming from a vicious circle in which extractive institutions reinvigorate themselves, so persistence alone proves nothing about correction.</li></ul><p>The defensible version is narrower: institutions that survive <em>and</em> remain prosperous under changing conditions tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors.</p><h4>Homeostasis</h4><p>A contrast can be drawn between good self-corrections and the idea of homeostasis in biology. An instruction can be analogized to be a single person, where our internal system is able to self-correct only with the need for external stimuli. On the other hand, good institutions are ones that are able to self-correct without the need for external stimuli, mostly because such external stimuli is almost always negative and harmful for the overall stability of the institution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>This note is a specific example of the broader pattern identified in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>Good institutions don’t do this? Perhaps self-correction allows the flaw to be minimized.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>Important for the design of good institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>Perhaps whether an institution has self-correcting mechanisms can affect the level of trust in that institution.</li></ul></li></ul>","TIPS yields are not a good proxy for real interest rates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Treasury Inflation Protected Securities are generally not an accurate proxy for the real interest rate.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a common misconception regarding TIPS.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The common reasoning is that since TIPS are adjusted for inflation, that would mean that it represents the true, real interest rate of the government. However, this logic is flawed because of two factors:</p><ol><li>Large institutional investors (e.g., pension funds) are often forced by the government to buy TIPS, which artificially drives down the yield.</li><li>Investors typically demand a liquidity premium on TIPS, since they are harder to transact than other bonds. </li></ol><p>These two factors combined means that, depending on which factor is stronger at a particular moment, they could radically misrepresent the true real interest rate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank\">Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank</a><ul><li>(1) in this note may be an example of government manipulation of the interest rates.</li></ul></li></ul>","The House of Representatives is a more populist institution compared to the Senate":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The House of Representative is a more populist institution since its members are more numerous and are elected from smaller districts. This means that they more directly represent the interests of their constituents. The Senate, on the other hand, represents larger populations, and lacks many of the rules that constrain the House, most notably the ability to end filibustering. This is since the Senate is considered a more deliberative body.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the difference between the two chambers of the US Congress.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The committee system in Congress enables specialization and increases efficiency\">The committee system in Congress enables specialization and increases efficiency</a><ul><li>This is a system that employed in Congress. Specifically, this connects to the Joint committee, which is where members from both houses meet together.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself\">Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself</a><ul><li>This is characterized by this note, since it describes the differences between the two branches.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Precipice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A wide-ranging argument that humanity faces an unprecedented existential risk in the 21st century, but also has a real chance to avert catastrophe if it acts wisely. Ord examines major threats such as nuclear war, engineered pandemics, unaligned artificial intelligence, and other “existential risks,” then explains why their combined danger is alarmingly high compared with past centuries. The book’s core message is both urgent and hopeful: we are living at a uniquely fragile moment in history, and by investing in risk reduction, global cooperation, and better long-term decision-making, we can protect the future of civilization and the vast potential of humanity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Ord argues that, if you do not take the problem of existential risks serious, we may face a permanent loss of our potential, which would be very bad.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time\">Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise\">Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should never halt technological innovation\">We should never halt technological innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem\">Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity\">Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Variability Hypothesis states that males exhibit a wider spread in many traits":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Variability Hypothesis essentially states that males have a greater range in terms of their trait exhibition (ex. IQ, physical traits, personality, spatial reasoning) compared to females. This may be due to how makes are the heterogametic sex ($XY$ chromosomes).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has been used to explain many differences between the two sexes, though the theory is disputed.</p><h4>References</h4>","The intolerant must mostly be tolerated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the intolerant must mostly be tolerated, except in cases where the intolerance threatens free institutions and basic liberties.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents one of the main positions on the topic of whether to allow intolerant people in society.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Unless the intolerant people are overtly and actively threatening the free institutions of a society or the basic liberties of the citizens in that society, their rights ought to be protected just like any other person. Additionally, the intolerant have nothing to complain about, since they do not acknowledge the very principles of liberty that would protect them. The intolerant still have moral personalities (i.e., they are still potentially capable of acting in a moral manner) and therefore deserve rights. This view was held both my Rawls and Karl Popper, though Popper holds a somewhat more intolerant perspective than Rawls on the issue, i.e., his bar for what qualifies as “intolerance” is lower.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>Mills would reject any form of intolerance, even towards the intolerant.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
